s-zrsc6asgmhv8tnc.text:
A test declares its SpecTag:

- in TypeScript and JavaScript, by opening its name with it: `test("@spec s-… what it checks", …)`;
- in Python, by a mark on the test, `@pytest.mark.spec("@spec s-…")`;
- in a further language, as the description of that language says.

The plugin for pytest of the SpecSheriff tools, `pytest-specsheriff`, registers the mark. It also opens the name a run reports with the SpecTag.

s-xr5xxa9cxf2mq5u.text:
`sheriff review` hands the reviewer's input to a runner. The runner's CLI is given the schema of the verdict. It answers in JSON held to that schema.

The verdict passes or refuses the commit. It names the findings, each with its node, file, line and problem. `sheriff review` reads as an answer that cannot be read (§s-nyh9yk22ba6g77q):

- an answer that passes the commit with a finding;
- an answer that refuses the commit with none.

A runner is started:

- with none of the user's settings, memory or hooks;
- with a system prompt of its own;
- in an empty working directory;
- with every tool its command line can turn off turned off;
- as a program, never through a shell.

A CLI installed as a command script, as npm installs it on Windows, is started as the program and the arguments the script names.

A runner's environment holds only the variables that name:

- the search path of programs, the folders of the operating system and of the user, and the user's name;
- the locale;
- a proxy, the hosts it skips and the certificates to trust;
- where the runner's CLI keeps its sign-in, the sign-in itself, and the provider serving the model.

The runner `claude` is the Claude Code CLI in print mode. It asks for the model and the reasoning effort the settings below give. Where that model is overloaded or not offered, it falls back to the fallback model they give.

The runner `codex` is the Codex CLI in `codex exec`, with a Codex home of its own. That home is made in a temporary directory for the run and deleted after it. It holds a copy of the user's sign-in and nothing else.

The runner `codex` runs with:

- its sandbox read-only;
- its session not kept;
- no skill offered.

The runner `codex` asks for the model and the reasoning effort the settings below give. Where the model is empty, it asks for no model.

`sheriff review` reads the settings of each runner from the git configuration of the user who commits:

- `specsheriff.review.claudeModel`, `specsheriff.review.claudeFallbackModel` and `specsheriff.review.claudeEffort` for the runner `claude`;
- `specsheriff.review.codexModel` and `specsheriff.review.codexEffort` for the runner `codex`.

An empty `codexModel` stands for the model Codex chooses for the signed-in account.

A setting the configuration holds, empty or not, is confirmed. The hooks and the skills of either plugin ask for no setting the configuration holds.

The hooks and the skills write a setting only as the user whose configuration it is answers. Where the user answers with the default, they write it as any other value. The session start (§s-477gtdrkw34dzxw) and the skill `spec-init` (§s-zh2vcr3xjumppg5, §s-2pdy9gw2jrjvfb4) say when each setting is asked for.

The defaults are:

- for the runner `claude`, the model `opus` at the reasoning effort `high`, falling back to `sonnet`;
- for the runner `codex`, the model Codex chooses, at the reasoning effort `high`.

Where the configuration lacks a setting of the runner that runs, `sheriff review` takes that setting's default. With every review held so, it names each setting it took a default for and how the user confirms it.

The settings of the reviewer in `sheriff.config.json` are `review.runner` and `review.minutes`. `sheriff review` takes no model and no effort from that file.

`review.minutes` is the time after which a run is ended. Unless set, it is twenty minutes.

Unless `review.runner` is set, the runner is that of the plugin whose tools run `sheriff review`. The `agent` file the build writes beside `release` names that runner:

- `claude` for the plugin for Claude Code;
- `codex` for the plugin for Codex CLI.

Tools holding no such file take `claude`.

s-v4qus7raqnwzneb.text:
`sheriff approve <sid>...` appends to `specs/_approvals.jsonl` one line per node. The line holds the node's sid, the fingerprint of its wording and, as `by`, the email git is configured with. It sets the node's status to `approved`. Where the latest line for the node and that fingerprint is already an approval, it appends nothing and only sets the status. It refuses:

- a section and a domain's header;
- a node of the SpecSheriff guidelines the project takes;
- a node none of whose owners is the one approving, naming its owners;
- the rule that approves the specification, unless the one approving is one of the specification's owners (§s-qzthyh7yqvfhsa2).

An approval stands for a wording of a node where the latest line for that node and that wording's fingerprint is an approval. The latest line is the one standing last in `specs/_approvals.jsonl`.

`sheriff approve --withdraw <sid>...` withdraws the approval of each wording of the node for which one stands, whether the node holds that wording or not. For each such wording it appends a line. The line holds the node's sid, that wording's fingerprint, `withdrawn: true` and, as `by`, the email git is configured with. It makes the node a draft.

Where no approval stands for any wording of the node, it appends nothing. Anyone may withdraw an approval. `sheriff approve --withdraw` refuses:

- a section and a domain's header;
- a node of the SpecSheriff guidelines the project takes;
- the rule that approves the specification, unless the one withdrawing is one of the specification's owners (§s-qzthyh7yqvfhsa2).

The fingerprint is a SHA-256 hash of one canonical string. The string holds the node's title, text and reason and the values its NodeKind adds beside them. It holds neither the owners a node names nor what the reindex builds. In the string:

- the keys of an object are sorted;
- a list keeps its own order.

In that string the reason stands under the key `rationale`, the name earlier releases gave it. Earlier releases gave a field of type `one of` an empty list of its own values where the domains add its values. In that string such a field stands in that form.

An owner is named by an email.

s-n76wt2bkqdmh78x.text:
The agent's hooks for Codex stand in the project's `.codex/hooks.json`, where `sheriff init --agent codex` writes them (§s-7unxytshhc4md54). They are:

- one when a session starts;
- one before an edit made with `apply_patch`;
- one after such an edit or a shell command.

Each holds only the lookup of the plugin for Codex CLI in Codex's cache (§s-4mej6ccn7xu3d3p) and a call to the plugin's script for that hook: `hooks/session-start.sh`, `hooks/guard.sh` or `hooks/after-edit.sh`. Where a hook finds no plugin, it does nothing.

The scripts do what the agent's hooks do (§s-477gtdrkw34dzxw). In a repository holding no `sheriff.config.json`, they do nothing.

Before an edit, `sheriff guard`:

- reads from the patch each file it adds, updates, deletes or moves;
- applies the patch to those files in memory, a moved file taken as deleted at its old path and added at its new one;
- judges each file as an edit made with the file tools (§s-st6td4mua9uqt9z).

It refuses the whole patch where:

- it refuses one of the patch's files;
- it cannot read or apply the patch.

s-suv42g8r3ra2h6b.text:
From each RecordType, `sheriff types` writes a type into each file of types and a definition under `$defs` into each file of RuntimeChecks. It takes the nodes in the order the specification reads:

- its files in the code-point order of their paths;
- the nodes of each file in the order they stand.

Every type and definition is named by the RecordType's name with the first letter of each word raised and the spaces dropped. Each type carries a SpecTag with the sid of its RecordType, as a symbol of code carries one with the rule it implements (§s-5z5gnu7pahr82xp).

A type and a definition hold, in this order:

- the fields of every RecordType above the node along its `refines` links, the topmost first;
- the node's own fields.

A variant's own fields stand instead where the rule on RecordType variants places them (§s-nk8td3ez58gahc4).

Each field keeps its name and the order its node gives it. It is required where its node says so and optional otherwise.

A field of type `record` refers to the type of that RecordType by its name. The object of a field of type `variant` is written out in full. Where the RecordType refuses a field nobody declared, its definition refuses any property its fields do not declare.

A name cannot be generated where:

- it is not an identifier in the language of its file;
- the file already uses it, the names the file imports and those its language builds in included.

In TypeScript a type is written `export type <name> = {...}`.

In Python:

- a type is written `<name> = TypedDict("<name>", {...})`;
- every name a type uses is imported from `typing`;
- a reference to another type is written as a string;
- an optional field is marked `NotRequired`;
- the object of a field of type `variant` is given a type of its own.

That type of its own is named by the type it stands in followed by the field's name with its first letter raised. It carries a SpecTag with the same sid.
